  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 144 PM EDT Tue Oct 27 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis/Forecast: Early this afternoon, while mid-level \nridging is evident across the Great Lakes, primary focus revolves \naround incoming shortwave that's currently across the Dakotas into \nMN. An attendant weak surface reflection is expected to shift \nnortheast into Ontario this evening, aiding to continue to drag a \nlow-level baroclinic zone northward across northern MI. An uptick in \nforcing for ascent ahead of this wave may prove to be enough to \nsqueeze out light showers across parts of the area into tonight.\nThis wave departs east on Wednesday, with a warm front lifting \nnorth across northern MI during the morning hours, setting the \nstage for another round of above normal temperatures. Shower \nchances return late Wednesday night - Thursday as another area \nof low pressure treks by to our north, dragging a cold front \nacross the area locally. Additional shower chances may present \nthemselves at times over the Halloween weekend, with the highest\nchances likely holding off until next week. \n\nForecast Details: At least low shower chances return to parts of \nnorthern MI later this afternoon and evening, potentially lingering \noff/on at times into a chunk of tonight. These chances generally \nM-32 and north, and really more of a nuisance than anything, as\nany meaningful QPF chances are low. These chances largely depart\noff to the north and east by 09-12z Wednesday. \n\nAnother warm up for Wednesday under partly to mostly sunny skies. \nHigh temperatures favored in the 60s area-wide with a handful of low \n70s likely across northern lower. This places at least a few climate\nsites within a few degrees of daily high temp records (ANJ, \nPLN, GLR, HTL -- see table below for existing record highs). \nWhile Thursday - Sunday high temperatures will be cooler than \nWednesday, above normal temperatures are expected to continue \n-- generally ranging from the mid-upper 50s north and low-mid \n60s south.\n\n  October 28 High Temperature Records\n    Location   |    High Temp (Year)\n      ANJ      |      64 F (1924)\n      PLN      |      71 F (1955)\n      GLR      |      70 F (1955)\n      APN      |      75 F (1989)\n      HTL      |      73 F (1989)\n      TVC      |      80 F (1927)\n\nShower chances return late Wednesday night into Thursday as a cold \nfront crosses northern MI. While expected coverage of these showers \ncontinues to wane a bit, they still should be pretty numerous late \nWednesday night across sections of eastern upper and northwest \nlower. Perhaps even a rumble of thunder for far northwest lower. \nOverall coverage expected to wane further with eastern extent \nThursday morning before chances exit stage right through Thursday \nafternoon. Expansive high pressure drifts in Thursday night \nthrough at least the first half of Saturday/Halloween day -- \nyielding quieter weather with continued above normal \ntemperatures. Some PoPs do return later in the day Saturday, but\nnot a lot of confidence in that at this point. Somewhat higher \nchances for showers Sunday, and even more so at times next week.",
